---
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import GuideCard from '../../components/GuideCard.astro';

const guides = (await getCollection('constructionEstimatingGuides')).sort((a,b) => a.data.order - b.data.order);
---
<BaseLayout title="Construction Estimating Guides, Spreadsheet & Calculator" description="Practical construction estimating guides plus a free contractor markup calculator and a Microsoft Excel estimating spreadsheet covering scope, takeoff, labour, subcontractors, bid review and job costing.">
  <Breadcrumbs items={[{name:'Home',href:'/'},{name:'Construction Estimating'}]}/>
  <header class="page-head wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">CONSTRUCTION ESTIMATING</p>
    <h1>Construction Estimating Guides, Calculator &amp; Excel System</h1>
    <p class="lede">Build the estimate in a deliberate order: define the scope, structure the cost record, measure traceable quantities, price current inputs, load the business's chosen cost-recovery method, review the result, submit a defined offer, and learn from what happens.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="section section-tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <h2>Make the number traceable before trying to make it precise</h2>
        <p>A spreadsheet can calculate exactly and still represent an incomplete job. These guides focus on the assumptions, responsibilities, quantities, hours, quotations, and review checks behind the final price.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hub-steps">
        <article><h3>Scope</h3><p>Define included work, exclusions, items to clarify, work by others, and responsibility at trade and supply/install interfaces before detailed measurement.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Measure</h3><p>Keep the source, calculation, unit, gross and net quantities, and controlling dimensions visible so takeoff can be checked and revised.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Price</h3><p>Separate measured quantity from purchasing quantity, build labor from person-hours, and compare subcontract quotations on comparable scope.</p></article>
        <article><h3>Review</h3><p>Challenge scope, quantities, labor, quotations, commercial calculations, documents, and fragile assumptions before the price leaves the business.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head">
        <p class="eyebrow">CHOOSE THE RIGHT TOOL</p>
        <h2>Choose a free calculator or the complete Excel toolkit</h2>
        <p>Use the free calculator when the cost base is already known. Use PRO when the estimate itself needs a connected workflow from scope through job costing and post-job learning.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-3"><article class="card"><p class="eyebrow">FREE JOB PROFIT CHECK</p><h3>Construction Job Profit Calculator</h3><p>Compare revenue with actual or forecast final costs and check margin, markup and contribution after overhead.</p><p><a class="button button-secondary" href="/tools/construction-job-profit-calculator/">Calculate job profit free</a></p><p><a href="/construction-job-costing/">Learn the construction job-costing workflow →</a></p></article>
        <article class="card">
          <p class="eyebrow">FREE CALCULATOR</p>
          <h3>Contractor Markup &amp; Margin Calculator</h3>
          <p>Enter a cost base and either a markup or target margin to calculate the selling price, profit, markup and true margin. It is a quick way to check the commercial arithmetic without confusing a 20% markup with a 20% margin.</p>
          <p><a class="button button-secondary" href="/tools/contractor-markup-margin-calculator/">Use the free calculator</a></p>
          <div class="notice"><strong>Arithmetic, not a recommended percentage.</strong> The calculator does not decide what your cost base should include or what commercial target your business should use.</div>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <p class="eyebrow">DOWNLOADABLE MICROSOFT EXCEL SYSTEM</p>
          <h3>Construction Estimating &amp; Job Costing Toolkit PRO</h3>
          <p>The PRO system carries the same estimating principles into a guided Excel workflow covering project setup, scope, takeoff, materials, labour, job costs, subcontractors, recovery, markup or margin, seven-pass review, client proposal and estimate-vs-actual learning.</p>
          <p><a class="button" href="/tools/construction-estimating-system-pro/">Explore the PRO estimating spreadsheet</a></p>
          <p class="byline">Complete v3.2 package: US$19.99 plus applicable tax, one-time purchase. Two core workbooks, demos, five bonuses, forms and guides. Microsoft Excel desktop.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">FREE PRACTICAL GUIDES</p><h2>Work through the parts of an estimate that most often hide assumptions</h2><p>Each guide is designed to stand alone while fitting the same scope-to-learning workflow used in the book and PRO workbook.</p></div>
      <div class="grid-3">{guides.map(guide => <GuideCard guide={guide} base="/construction-estimating/" label="CONSTRUCTION ESTIMATING" />)}</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="wrap narrow">
      <div class="section-head"><p class="eyebrow">FULL WORKED EXAMPLE</p><h2>Construction Estimating for Your First Jobs</h2></div>
      <p>The book follows the fictional Oakridge Workshop from project information and drawings through takeoff, purchasing quantities, labor, subcontract quotations, company-level treatment, red-team bid review, a customer-facing proposal, and post-job learning. The project figures are teaching inputs rather than current market prices or recommended rates.</p>
      <p><a class="button" href="/books/construction-estimating-for-your-first-jobs/">Explore the book</a></p>
      <div class="notice"><strong>Training example only.</strong> The Oakridge Workshop is fictional and is used to demonstrate estimating methods. It is not a construction design, price guide, or source of recommended rates.</div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
